---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import EntityReference from '../../components/EntityReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTML Character Entity Reference" description="Searchable reference for common HTML character entities — symbols, punctuation, and special characters.">
  <EntityReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why entities still matter in modern HTML</h2>
  <p>UTF-8 has made most special characters directly typeable in HTML source, but entities remain necessary for reserved characters like <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code>, and useful as a fallback when your editor or encoding setup doesn't handle a special character cleanly.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Search for the character or symbol you need.</li>
    <li>Click to copy its HTML entity code.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to escape <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code></strong> when displaying literal text that happens to contain these characters, which browsers otherwise interpret as markup.</li>
    <li><strong>Double-escaping</strong> — encoding an already-encoded entity, producing visible entity codes instead of the intended symbol.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Displaying a literal <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code> character in rendered HTML without it being parsed as a tag</li>
    <li>Adding a copyright, trademark, or currency symbol to a page</li>
    <li>Inserting a non-breaking space to prevent unwanted line breaks</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Do I need entities for all special characters now?</strong> Not for most — UTF-8 handles the vast majority directly; entities remain essential mainly for HTML's own reserved characters.</p>
</ToolLayout>
